o stay within the maximum. I've finalized the task groupings with 4 tasks respecting the weight limit, but I realize I'm working without the actual SRD requirement texts—I only have roadmap descriptions and status information. I need to reference the SRD file directly so the stitch agent can read the full requirement content and validate these groupings properly.\n\nLooking at how the tasks are structured, they reference specific SRD requirements in their titles like \"srd005-wc R2.1-R2.4\", which means the stitch agent will pull the actual requirement details from the SRD. For the task descriptions, I should list the specific requirement IDs and include the SRD file in required_reading, then keep the descriptions somewhat general since the agent will have access to the full requirement text. The challenge is balancing the planning constitution's demand for specificity with the reality that I don't have the requirement content itself—but the SRD reference should bridge that gap. I'm noticing a pattern in the completed tasks—each one covers exactly 4 requirements with a weight budget of 4, suggesting a consistent 1:1 weight ratio. Since I don't have access to the requirements.yaml file to verify the actual weights, I'll proceed with this assumption and note it as I propose the next task groupings.\n\nLooking at what's likely left for the wc implementation, I'm thinking through the remaining requirement groups: R4 might cover error handling and edge cases, R5 could address stdin and pipe operations, and R6 might involve differential testing or version/help functionality. I'll structure the task descriptions as instructions for the stitch agent, referencing the SRD directly.\n\nI'm also considering whether to propose tasks beyond wc, but given the constraint to focus on the earliest incomplete release and only having 4 tasks available against 14 remaining wc requirements, I should stick with wc. Since there's already code in cmd/wc/ from recently completed tasks, the r