ent IDs and let the descriptions speak to what each group likely covers.\n\nI need to make sure the stitch agent knows what's already implemented—R2.1-R2.6 and R3.1-R3.2 are done—so it doesn't overwrite existing code. Now I'm drafting the task descriptions, starting with R1.1-R1.4 as the first task, then moving into R3.3 and R4.1-R4.3 for the second task.\n\nSince all these tasks modify the same files, they'll need to be sequential to avoid conflicts. I'm checking whether the requirements are distinct enough in the codebase to allow parallel work, but it looks like they'll need to depend on each other in order.\n\nLet me think about whether there's a better way to group these requirements based on their actual content and dependencies, rather than just splitting them arbitrarily by requirement number. I'm checking the YAML structure now and realizing I need to make assumptions about weights since I don't have the actual requirement definitions. I'll proceed with weight-1 for most requirements and note that the orchestrator can reject and replan if the weights differ. Let me organize the remaining wc requirements into four tasks: the first covering basic structure and core functionality, the second handling output formatting and filtering, the third addressing performance and edge cases, and the fourth wrapping up remaining requirements. Now I need to write out each task with proper deliverables, required reading, and file specifications. I need to work with what's provided inline since I can't read files. Let me map out the wc functionality I know: line counting, word counting, byte and character counting, max line length, multi-file support with totals, stdin handling, flag combinations, output formatting, error handling for unreadable files, and version/help flags. Since R2.1-R2.4 and R2.5-R3.2 are already done, the remaining requirements probably cover basic input and counting operations in the R1 group, with R4-R6 likely addressing edge cases, error handling, and testing.\n\nI'm realizing I should stop overthinking this—the stitch agent will read the SRD, so I just need to provide solid task structure. Since Tasks 5177 and 5178 were completed, main.go and wc_test.go should already exist, which means I should mark these as modifications rather than new files. For the task titles, I'll follow the existing pattern with descriptive names and SRD references, though I'll need to be careful about the exact requirement content.\n\nThe key insight is that my task descriptions need to be self-contained for the stitch agent—I should specify which SRD to read, which requirements to implement, which files to modify, and what the acceptance criteria are, since the agent won't have access to my analysis.\n\nLooking at the existing state, the wc package already has main.go and wc_test.go with R2.1-R2.6 and R3.1-R3.2 implemented, so I need to instruct the agent to add to these files rather than replace them. The remaining R1 requirements appear to be foundational, and I should now write out the task specifications with the standard file conventions in mind.\n\nSince R1 is marked as ready in the orchestrator, I'll structure it as the first task to implement, followed by the remaining requirements. Let me lay out the task breakdown with their weights and dependencies.\n\nGenerating YAML output... I need to implement the remaining requirements R1.3 and R1.4 for the srd005-wc specification, while preserving all existing functionality and following the project's differential testing pattern with the SIGPIPE handler convention.\n\nLooking at the acceptance criteria, I need to ensure the new requirements pass differential tests against the gwc reference binary, that existing tests still pass, and the build succeeds without errors.\n\nBut I'm realizing the task requirement text is too generic—just \"Implement srd005-wc requirement R1.1 as specified in the SRD.\" The planning constitution says to be specific and actionable, with each SRD requirement mapping to exactly one task. However, without the actual SRD content, I can't be more specific than this. The stitch agent will read the SRD file from required_reading and implement accordingly, which is the standard pattern.\n\nLooking at similar completed tasks, they group related requirements in their titles—like \"flag selection and output formatting\" for R2.1-R2.4—but the actual task descriptions reference the SRD requirements directly. So my approach is correct: reference the SRD, list the specific requirement IDs, and let the stitch agent handle the implementation details.\n\nThe key information I need to include is which SRD to read, which requirement IDs to implement, which files to modify, and what patterns to follow like differential testing and SIGPIPE handling. I should also note that pkg/testutils/ and pkg/sys/ are needed for those patterns. Following the commit format, I'll anchor the title to the specific requirement IDs like \"(srd005-wc R2.1-R2.4)\".\n\nFor the requirement text fields, I'll reference the SRD IDs directly since the stitch agent can read the SRD. I know wc counts lines, words, bytes, characters, and max line length with flags -l, -w, -c, -m, -L, and outputs lines/words/bytes by default when no flags are specified. I'll use this to make the tasks more specific and actionable rather than just saying \"implement as specified.\" to be about basic functionality/structure. Looking at the patterns across other SRDs like echo and tac, each requirement group represents a distinct functional area. For wc with six groups, that suggests more functional areas than simpler utilities—R1 likely covers basic input/output and program structure, R2 handles flag selection and output formatting, R3 manages additional counting modes and multi-file totals, and R4 probably addresses the --files0-from option.\n\nThat leaves R5 for stdin and pipe handling, and R6 for error handling and exit codes. Before I finalize the output, I need to verify I have all the required fields: deliverable_type, required_reading, files with paths and actions, requirements with IDs and text, design decisions, and acceptance criteria. I should also check the requirements.yaml file to include the weights for each requirement I'm planning to implement.\n\nSince I can't access requirements.yaml directly, I'm assuming a default weight of 1 for all requirements. This aligns with how prior tasks grouped 4 requirements per task, and the wc SRD isn't in the restructured weight-4 list. With 14 total wc requirements and a max of 4 tasks at weight 4 each, I can fit all of them perfectly without needing to consider other releases. Let me verify the release priorities by checking which ones have incomplete work.\n\nLooking at the releases, I notice some have \"not started\" status but don't appear in requirement_states—like rel00.0-uc002-format and rel00.0-uc004-testutils. This seems contradictory. If requirement_states only tracks SRDs with ready requirements, then missing entries could mean either all requirements are already complete, they're in some other state like in_progress, or the SRD simply has no requirements.yaml entry at all.\n\nGiven the constraint that I can only propose tasks for items with \"ready\" status, and since these SRDs aren't listed in requirement_states, I can't propose tasks for them—even if they appear not started. The same applies to srd006-cat. Looking at what's actually available, rel03.0 with srd005 is the only release that has ready requirements I can work with. I'm reconsidering whether to include rel03.1 requirements alongside wc, but the 4-task limit means I should complete wc first and let the next run handle rel03.1. Now I'm working through the weight budget to confirm all 14 wc requirements fit across my 4 tasks, assigning one weight per requirement.\n\nI'm double-checking whether my grouping makes semantic sense, particularly since R3.3 crosses into the R4 group—the planning constitution warns against crossing requirement boundaries, but I also need to respect the 4-requirement-per-task maximum.\n\nR3.3 is a leftover from group 3, and I have limited options: either leave it alone as a single-requirement task (too small), combine it with R1.1-R1.4 (which would exceed the limit at 5 requirements), or pair it with R4.1-R4.3. The principle about not crossing boundaries seems to apply to splitting individual requirements, not keeping entire groups together, so combining R3.3 with R4.1-R4.3 feels justified.\n\nNow I'll write the final output with focused descriptions and let the SRD fill in the details.\n\nI notice the recent commits use `generation-run44` as the branch and task numbers like 5177, 5178 are assigned by the orchestrator, so I don't need to handle numbering myself. The commit titles follow a pattern like \"[stitch] cmd/wc: description (srd005-wc RIDS)\", though the output format just asks for a title, so I'll use descriptive titles with SRD references and let the orchestrator handle any prefixes.\n\nGenerating task output... I need to complete the requirements list and outline the design decisions and acceptance criteria for implementing the remaining features R1.3 and R1.4, while preserving existing functionality and following the differential testing pattern with gwc as the reference. I need to modify both the main implementation and test files to add support for the remaining requirements, specifically implementing R3.3 and the R4 series features as outlined in the specification. Implementing srd005-wc R4.2 and R4.3 as specified, while preserving all existing functionality and following the differential testing pattern with gwc as the reference. The acceptance criteria cover implementing requirements R3.3 through R4.3 in cmd/wc/main.go, passing differential tests against gwc, ensuring all existing tests pass, and verifying the build succeeds. Next I'm moving on to handle the edge cases and extended behavior for R4.4, R5.1-R5.2, and R6.1, which will require modi